```js
export function solve(n, edges) {
  const degree = Array(n).fill(0);
  const out = [];

  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    degree[b]++;
  }

  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (degree[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  while (q.length) {
    q.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const u = q.shift();
    out.push(u);

    for (const [a, b] of edges) {
      if (a === u) {
        degree[b]--;
        if (degree[b] === 0) q.push(b);
      }
    }
  }

  return out.length === n ? out : [];
}
```

The fix changes the completion check from `n - 1` to `n`: success requires every vertex to be emitted. Each remaining indegree counts edges from unprocessed vertices, and selecting the smallest zero-indegree vertex preserves the lexicographically smallest ordering; if processing stops before all vertices are emitted, a cycle remains.

Use the existing synchronous `solve(n, edges)` export or service wrapper; no dependencies, API changes, or test edits are needed.

